mn is removed because its categorical values—ByBirth, ByOtherMeans, Temporary—do not provide sufficient predictive power or direct insight into credit card approval likelihood. Citizenship status often has indirect effects on creditworthiness and is better represented by other features such as BankCustomer or Income.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['Citizen'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column DriversLicense is dropped\n# The DriversLicense column is removed because it has weak correlations to factors that directly influence credit card approval, like income or default history. Having a driver's license may not significantly affect credit card approval chances.\n\ndf.drop(columns=['DriversLicense'], inplace=True)\n```end", "### Step 1: Add a feature analyzing the debt burden adjusted by employment stability\n```python\n# Debt_Burden_Adjusted\n# Feature description: The product of 'Debt_to_Income_Ratio' and 'Employment_Stability_Score'. This captures an individual's debt burden weighted by their employment stability, providing a nuanced measure for assessing creditworthiness.\n# Usefulness: Combines the financial aspects (debt burden relative to income) with life stability (employment consistency) to better gauge the individual's likelihood of credit card approval. A higher employment stability could outweigh higher debt-to-income ratios in some scenarios.\n\ndf['Debt_Burden_Adjusted'] = df['Debt_to_Income_Ratio'] * df['Employment_Stability_Score']\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Add a categorical transformation for credit score performance\n```python\n# CreditScore_Category\n# Feature description: Bin 'CreditScore' to categorical groups such as 'Low', 'Medium', 'High', and 'Excellent', representing the credit health tiers.\n# Usefulness: Many credit decisions function on tiered credit scores rather than raw numerical values. This simplifies downstream tasks related to credit scoring and facilitates credit card success prediction.\n\nbins = [-1, 1, 1